{"kind":"report","text":"1c0efc81 is satisfied. The sorting-scratch reservation and the shared attachment ceiling merged as 471a145a and are pushed.\n\nOne mechanism reserves the encoder name-sorting storage: it preflights before allocation, holds the charge through the event write and releases it after, and both construction encoding paths go through it. The preflight now takes the concrete chunk writer, so it counts the retained output capacity and the headroom its growth copy needs — the blind spot your last verdict named, which existed because the helper had only an io.Writer and could not see the buffer in principle.\n\nOne named constant is the attachment ceiling for both the compact writer and its decoder, checked before any byte is emitted and before the names slice is allocated, so no checkpoint can be emitted that its own decoder refuses.\n\nThe name-length asymmetry I found while checking that is worth carrying forward: the writer accepts names to 65535 and the decoder accepts 1 to 128, and it is unreachable only because internal/gitstore validates every attachment name against a 1-to-128-character pattern at the store boundary. The guarantee rests on gitstore rather than on the compact writer, so a change to that pattern would separate encoder from decoder without either file being touched. That is now in the merged artifact rather than only in my head.","body":{"commit":"471a145a779f10fed5c0ae5611c79a7acdbcc45f"}}